 "result": "This repo is a Flask-like framework called `tessel`. Everything is in `src/tessel/views.py`.\n\n1. **Base class `View`** (line 16). It has no `as_view`. The classmethod `View.to_view_func(name, *class_args, **class_kwargs)` (line 86) builds the view function.\n   - The inner `view(**kwargs)` is a closure. It creates `view.view_class(*class_args, **class_kwargs)` on each request, or reuses one shared instance when `init_every_request = False`.\n   - It then calls `active_app.ensure_sync(self.run_view)(**kwargs)`.\n   - It applies any `decorators`, then sets `view.view_class`, `__name__`, `__doc__`, `view.methods` and `view.provide_automatic_options` (lines 118–134). The router reads `view.methods` to know which HTTP methods the route accepts.\n\n2. **Subclass `VerbView(View)`** (line 138). This is the class that routes `get()`, `post()` and the other verbs.\n   - `VerbView.__init_subclass__` (line 165) runs when you define a subclass. It checks which names in `http_method_funcs` (line 11) the class has, such as `get` and `post`. It sets `cls.methods` to the upper-cased names, plus any inherited from base classes. If the subclass sets `methods` itself, it skips this.\n   - `VerbView.run_view` (line 182) is what the generated view function calls. It looks up `getattr(self, request.method.lower())` and falls back to `get` for `HEAD` when there is no `head` handler. It then calls the handler through `ensure_sync` with the URL kwargs.\n\nYou register the result with `app.attach_route_rule(\"/path\", view_func=MyView.to_view_func(\"name\"))`.",
